        "EU rules on carbon dioxide emissions from new cars and vans do not set a limit for each vehicle. Instead, they set targets for the average emissions of all the new vehicles that a manufacturer registers in the EU in a given year. A manufacturer's individual target depends partly on the average mass of its fleet, so a producer of heavier cars is allowed somewhat higher average emissions than one selling mainly small models. Manufacturers may also form pools and be assessed together, which lets a company with few zero-emission models combine its figures with one that sells many.\n\nA manufacturer whose average exceeds its target pays an excess emissions premium of €95 for each gram per kilometre above the target, multiplied by the number of new vehicles it registered that year. Because the premium applies across the whole fleet, even a small excess can become expensive for a large producer.\n\nThe 2023 revision of the rules tightened the targets for 2030 to a 55% cut for cars and a 50% cut for vans, compared with 2021 levels. In 2025, after carmakers warned that they faced heavy penalties, the EU allowed manufacturers to show compliance with their 2025 targets on the basis of their average performance over 2025, 2026 and 2027, rather than for 2025 alone. Critics argued that the change rewarded firms that had been slow to invest.",
