        "Revised EU rules on the rights of rail passengers have applied since June 2023. When a train arrives at the final destination 60 to 119 minutes late, passengers are entitled to compensation of 25% of the ticket price; for a delay of two hours or more, the figure rises to 50%. Railway undertakings may set a minimum threshold, of no more than €4, below which compensation is not paid. Passengers who were already informed of a delay before they bought their ticket have no right to compensation for it.\n\nThe revision added a new exception. A carrier no longer owes compensation if the delay is caused by extraordinary circumstances such as extreme weather, a major natural disaster or a major public health crisis, provided that it could not have been avoided. Strikes by the carrier's own staff, however, do not count as extraordinary circumstances. The exception concerns compensation only: it does not remove the carrier's duties to inform and assist passengers and to offer them rerouting.\n\nIf the carrier does not communicate rerouting options within 100 minutes of the scheduled departure time, passengers may arrange alternative public transport themselves and claim back the necessary, appropriate and reasonable costs. Member States may exempt urban, suburban and regional services from parts of the regulation.",
